#version 450
//! `S[h][i][j] = scale * sum_d Q[h][d][i] * K[h][d][j]`, the first half of attention.
//!
//! Two uses per transformer block in PP-OCRv5 recognition, and Supertonic's text encoder
//! attends its text over 50 style tokens with it.
//!
//! # Q and K may be different lengths
//!
//! The output is `[heads, queries, keys]`, square only for self-attention. `p.out_h` is Q's
//! length and `p.out_w` is K's, so the two sequences are strided independently; when they are
//! equal this is the arithmetic it always was.
//!
//! # The contraction is over the middle axis, and that is the whole point
//!
//! Q and K arrive as `[d_model, 1, T]` — the layout the CNN backbone already produces,
//! see `nets::Kind::LayerNorm` for why this runtime keeps it. Read as `[heads, head_dim,
//! T]` those are the same bytes in the same order, so a head is a run of channels and
//! splitting `d_model` into heads costs nothing: no `Permute`, no `Reshape`, no copy.
//! `p.group` carries the head count and this indexes accordingly.
//!
//! What it costs is that the reduction walks `head_dim` channels a stride apart rather
//! than a contiguous vector. At `head_dim` 15 that is 15 strided pairs per score. The
//! alternative — transposing to `[T, d_model]` so each head's vector is contiguous —
//! buys a contiguous read here and pays two permutes of the whole sequence per
//! attention, twice per block.
//!
//! # One invocation per score
//!
//! The output is `[heads, queries, keys]`, so there are `heads * queries * keys` invocations
//! and each reduces `head_dim` products. That is the parallel axis: `T` is around 80 for a line
//! of text, so this is tens of thousands of independent invocations against `head_dim`
//! serial steps each - the opposite balance to `gap.comp`, which has only channels to
//! spread across.
//!
//! # Scale
//!
//! `1 / sqrt(head_dim)`, arriving as raw `f32` bits in `p.param0_bits`. It is derived
//! from the head geometry by `nets::Builder::attn_scores` rather than read from the
//! weights file, because it is not a trained value — it is a property of the shape.
//!
//! Applied here, to the accumulator, rather than to Q's projection weights: folding it
//! there would work and would make the scale invisible in the one place a reader looks
//! to check it.

#include "common.glsl"

void main() {
    uint index = global_index();
    if (index >= p.count) {
        return;
    }
    // The output is `[heads, queries, keys]`, so `unpack` gives head, query and key directly.
    uint head, query, key;
    unpack(index, head, query, key);
    // Q and K are `[d_model, 1, T]`: one channel every `T` elements. A head owns `head_dim`
    // consecutive channels starting at `head * head_dim`. The two lengths are the score map's
    // own height and width.
    uint head_dim = p.in_c / p.group;
    uint query_stride = p.out_h;
    uint key_stride = p.out_w;
    uint query_base = head * head_dim * query_stride;
    uint key_base = head * head_dim * key_stride;
    float total = 0.0;
    for (uint d = 0u; d < head_dim; d++) {
        total += float(arena[p.in0 + query_base + d * query_stride + query])
               * float(arena[p.in1 + key_base + d * key_stride + key]);
    }
    arena[p.out_at + index] = float16_t(total * uintBitsToFloat(p.param0_bits));
}
